schema: encyclopedia-entry
object:
  title: "Centralized vs. Decentralized Systems: What It Means for Your Garden and Community"
  type: encyclopedia-entry
  page_type: comparison
  summary: >-
    A foundations-track explainer contrasting centralized systems (one
    authority in charge — banks, concentrated food-processing, single-
    telecom mobile money) against decentralized systems (control spread
    across many independent parts — cooperatives, rotating savings circles,
    community seed banks, Bitcoin), aimed at community gardeners and
    permaculturists new to crypto. Argues most real systems are hybrids on a
    spectrum, ties the distinction to permaculture's resilience-through-
    diversity principle, and cites a 2022 food-systems resilience study as
    supporting evidence while noting the same study found centralization can
    help during labor shortages.
  audience: individual community gardeners/permaculturists new to web3
  known_tensions:
    - >-
      centralized systems are easier to manage and can produce more cheaply
      (a single large farm vs many small ones) but carry a single point of
      failure; decentralized systems trade some efficiency for resilience.
    - >-
      the article's own cited study found decentralization improves
      resilience under supply-chain disruption but centralization can be
      favored when responding to labor shortages — presented as evidence
      that neither approach is categorically better.
  related_concepts:
    - centralization-decentralization-spectrum
  related_resources:
    - Bankless Academy
  maturity: raw
  public_use: source-linked-unreviewed
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/centralized-vs-decentralized.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
  work_order: wo-a42af3f6559f
  source_lineage: centralized-vs-decentralized.md
  notes: |
    # assessed-but-excluded
    - Satoshi Nakamoto is referenced only as Bitcoin's creator/motivation
      (a widely public, already-documented historical fact, consistent with
      this batch's bitcoin-history.md ingestion) — not modeled as a public
      person-node, no high-risk trigger.
    - No exact locations, TEK/Indigenous knowledge specifics, MRV/carbon
      claims, or funding/legal/governance recommendations present; the
      article's mentions of Ethiopian/Kenyan cooperatives and savings
      circles (ekub, chamas) are used only as brief, already-widely-known
      illustrative examples, not attributed practices requiring the deeper
      cultural-attribution review applied to building-momentum.md elsewhere
      in this batch.
